# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Why the base under a rental yard is built like any other lawn, just on a schedule",
         "<p>The base under a {city} vacation rental's turf is not a different build from a plain family lawn: sod and a few inches of soil come out, then two to four inches of washed, open-graded crushed rock go down and compact firm over native sand left loose enough to keep draining. What changes is timing. That compacted rock layer benefits from a day or two to settle before turf goes on top, and on a rental that day has to land inside a gap between checkout and the next arrival rather than whenever it happens to suit the crew, which shrinks the calendar window more than the build itself does.</p>"),
        ("Grading a side yard to match a pool deck someone already built",
         "<p>Many {city} rental homes already have a paved pool deck in place before a side-yard turf job ever gets scheduled, and the new base has to grade into whatever slope that existing deck established rather than starting from a blank lot. Getting the transition wrong leaves a low spot right where the two surfaces meet, which is exactly where a rolling cooler or a suitcase crosses from the yard to the patio. Matching the base's one to two percent grade to an existing structure takes more field measuring than laying out a border on open ground with no prior construction to work around.</p>"),
        ("Extra compaction passes on the path guests actually take",
         "<p>A rental yard around {city} rarely gets even foot traffic the way a family lawn does; almost everyone crosses the same stretch between the driveway and the pool cage door, week after week, regardless of how big the lot is. Running a few extra compaction passes on that specific strip before turf goes down keeps the base firm exactly where repeated wheeled luggage and coolers will put the most pressure, rather than compacting the whole yard to one uniform standard and hoping the busiest section holds up the same as a corner nobody walks.</p>"),
        ("Why do drainage problems on a vacant rental lot go unnoticed longer?",
         "<p>A base that drains too slowly shows up the same way on any {city} property, standing water and a soft spot after a storm, but a rental home sitting empty between bookings has nobody around to notice it for days or weeks at a time. That gap between when a base problem starts and when anyone sees it is the strongest argument for building the full two to four inches of washed rock correctly the first time, rather than assuming a minor shortcut will get caught and fixed before it matters. By the time a guest mentions a soggy corner in a review, the base has usually been failing quietly for a while.</p>"),
        ("Keeping runoff on the lot in a tightly packed rental subdivision",
         "<p>Resort-belt rental subdivisions around {city} often place homes closer together than an older neighborhood would, so the state's rule against increasing runoff onto an adjoining property carries more practical weight here than it would on a large, spread-out lot. Washed, open-graded crushed rock keeps fines from binding into a crust that would otherwise shed water faster off the surface, and the one to two percent grade gets planned with the neighbor's fence line specifically in mind rather than just aimed at the nearest low point. A rental home's drainage plan affects the property next door more directly than a standalone rural lot's would.</p>"),
        ("A corner-lot rental's two exits, worked into a tighter calendar",
         "<p>A {city} rental sitting on a corner lot faces the same two-street-frontage grading question any corner lot does, splitting the one to two percent fall toward two separate exit points rather than one, but a rental's narrow gap between checkout and the next arrival leaves less room to come back and correct a grading mistake later than an owner-occupied job would. Getting both exits right during the original compaction, rather than planning to revisit one side after the season's first storm exposes a problem, matters more here simply because the calendar rarely offers a convenient second visit.</p>"),
        ("When the booking calendar decides the base gets compacted a little wet",
         "<p>A {city} rental's vacancy window sometimes lands during a stretch of rain that would make a homeowner reschedule an ordinary lawn job without a second thought, but a fixed date between two bookings doesn't always leave that option. Compacting rock that's wetter than ideal produces a less consistent base than a dry-weather pass would, which is why a crew working inside a rain-affected rental window sometimes adds an extra compaction pass to compensate rather than accepting a single pass on marginal ground. It is a compromise driven by the calendar rather than the weather, and it is worth knowing about before assuming every rental base was built under ideal conditions.</p>"),
        ("Keeping standing water off a privacy fence's base rail",
         "<p>Many {city} rental properties sit inside communities bordered by a privacy fence rather than open space, and turf graded right up against that fence's base rail without a slight fall away from it can leave water sitting against the rail after every storm, which shortens the life of the fence itself rather than the turf. Building a small amount of fall away from the fence line into the base, on top of the yard's overall one to two percent grade, keeps that specific edge from becoming the one spot on the property that stays damp between rinses and storms.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Why is lifespan harder to predict on a rental than on a family lawn?",
         "<p>Synthetic turf generally lasts ten to twenty years depending on UV stabilization, traffic, and infill, and a {city} rental home's wear pattern makes that range harder to predict than it is for one household's yard, since a rotating set of strangers puts different demands on the surface week to week: some groups barely touch the yard, others drag furniture across it daily. Picking a denser, better-stabilized product going in is less about matching one family's habits and more about building in a margin for whichever kind of booking shows up most, since there is no way to select tenants the way an owner would choose their own use pattern.</p>"),
        ("Infill for a listing that markets itself as pet-friendly",
         "<p>A {city} rental advertised as pet-friendly needs its infill chosen for a rotating set of guest dogs rather than one household's pet: zeolite or a coated silica sand for odor control, the same natural-material options the state's turf standard allows on any single-family lot, never a rubber or synthetic fill outside playground equipment. A listing that does not specifically market to pet owners can use plain silica sand instead, since occasional guest pets put far less demand on odor control than a dog living there year-round would. The listing description itself is often the clearest signal for which infill actually makes sense.</p>"),
        ("Pricing just the driveway strip instead of the whole yard",
         "<p>Installed turf runs eight to eighteen dollars a square foot, typically ten to sixteen, and that range does not shift by {city} address, only by base condition and access. An owner who wants to start small might turf only the narrow strip along the driveway where guests wheel luggage across dying grass, say 150 sq ft: at the typical range, that comes out to somewhere between roughly fifteen hundred and twenty-four hundred dollars, a fraction of a full-yard job. Phasing a rental property this way lets an owner cover the section guests actually photograph first and expand later.</p>"),
        ("Why the backing has to perform without anyone checking on it",
         "<p>A permeable backing built to pass more than thirty inches of water an hour matters on any {city} property, but a rental home puts a sharper edge on that requirement, since a slow or partly clogged backing might go unnoticed for an entire booking season with nobody living there to catch standing water early. Choosing a fully specified backing rather than a discount product that barely clears the drainage standard is less about performance on day one and more about the margin it leaves for months of unattended use between an owner's occasional visits.</p>"),
        ("Why green is the only color decision the state rule addresses",
         "<p>Florida's turf standard specifically allows green synthetic turf, which settles the one product question that would otherwise vary from job to job on a {city} rental: color stays consistent across every listing photo taken over the years, regardless of season or which crew handled a repair. That consistency matters more for a property marketed through rotating photos on a booking site than it does for a family home nobody photographs professionally. Beyond color, the product decisions that actually vary, pile density, infill type, backing spec, get made the same way they would for any residential lot.</p>"),
        ("Why a rental's product paperwork matters more after a change of hands",
         "<p>Documentation showing a {city} rental's turf was installed to its specific manufacturer's specifications, correct infill rate, correct adhesive, correct backing weight, matters more here than on an owner-occupied home, since a rental property can change management companies or even ownership within the turf's ten- to twenty-year lifespan, and whoever inherits the property afterward has no memory of what was actually installed. Keeping that paperwork with the property's other records, rather than only with the original owner personally, means a future manager troubleshooting a problem has something to check the surface against instead of guessing at the original spec.</p>"),
        ("Choosing a heavier infill grain because nobody's watching it wash away",
         "<p>The state's rule against infill washing off the property is harder to catch a violation of on a {city} rental than on a home with residents walking the yard daily, since a lighter grain drifting toward a low corner during a storm might go unnoticed for weeks between cleanings rather than being caught the same afternoon. Choosing a slightly heavier grain of silica or coated sand for a rental's product spec, purely because of that unattended gap, costs little extra and reduces how much infill actually has a chance to migrate before anyone's there to notice or correct it.</p>"),
        ("Keeping the same product spec across a rental portfolio",
         "<p>A property manager overseeing several {city} rental homes benefits from specifying the same backing weight, infill type, and infill rate across every property in the portfolio rather than letting each job default to whatever a given crew happens to install that week, since predictable maintenance across a portfolio depends on knowing every yard behaves the same way under the same care routine. A single infill type also simplifies buying it in bulk for top-ups rather than tracking which product went into which address. This kind of consistency is worth specifying in writing before the first property in a portfolio gets turfed.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Building a rain-day buffer into a rainy-season install",
         "<p>Scheduling a {city} rental yard's turf install between June and September means planning around afternoon storms that can turn a graded, uncompacted base to mud in an hour, so the calendar gets an extra day or two of slack built in beyond what a dry-season job of the same size would need. Losing a scheduled day to weather during rainy season is common enough that promising a hard finish date without that buffer risks pushing the job into the next booking rather than finishing inside the gap it was planned around. A dry-season job books tighter to the calendar with less padding required.</p>"),
        ("Who hands over the gate code before anyone shows up to work?",
         "<p>Most {city} rental jobs start with a lockbox code or a gate code handed over by a property manager days before the crew arrives, rather than someone meeting them on site the way an owner-occupied job usually goes. Confirming that access actually works, and that nothing has changed on the manager's end, the morning before a scheduled start avoids losing the first hour of a booking-constrained job to a wrong code or a locked side gate. This handoff happens through whoever holds the calendar, which is not always the same person who signed the original contract.</p>"),
        ("Extra nails where the luggage crosses, not everywhere",
         "<p>Rather than tightening perimeter spacing across an entire {city} rental yard, the corner where the entry path meets the driveway, the specific spot suitcases and coolers roll across on their way in, gets the closest nail spacing on the job. The rest of the perimeter, along a side fence nobody drags anything across, holds to a standard residential spacing. Concentrating the extra anchoring on one identifiable stretch does more for a rental's actual wear pattern than spreading a uniform tightened spacing across corners that will barely see foot traffic at all.</p>"),
        ("Putting the capped irrigation zone in writing",
         "<p>Once a sprinkler zone under a {city} rental's new turf gets capped at the valve, that change goes into a short written note left with the property manager rather than just being mentioned on site, since the person managing the yard afterward is rarely the same person who watched the work happen. The note names which zone is now dry and points to the hose bib nearest the new turf for whatever rinsing the cleaning crew ends up doing. A lawn contractor still billing for a zone that no longer waters anything is a common, avoidable gap on a property nobody visits daily.</p>"),
        ("Working around whatever other crew is already booked that week",
         "<p>A {city} rental turf job often lands in the same vacancy window as a screen-cage repair, a paint touch-up, or another exterior project a property manager has already scheduled around the same gap in bookings, since owners try to bundle work into one closed stretch rather than losing separate weekends throughout the year. Coordinating start times with whoever else is on site that week, and staying clear of a freshly painted screen frame or a cage under repair, takes a short phone call before work begins rather than discovering a scheduling conflict once two crews show up the same morning.</p>"),
        ("Who actually does the first walkthrough when the owner lives elsewhere",
         "<p>On a {city} rental where the owner lives out of state, the crew's finished-day walkthrough often gets handed to whoever locally represents the property, a cleaning crew supervisor or the property manager, rather than the owner who commissioned the job. Walking that person through what a flat seam, a properly anchored edge, and even infill actually look like takes a few extra minutes on install day but means the first person to notice a problem later actually knows what normal looks like. Skipping this handoff conversation leaves a local contact unable to tell a real issue from ordinary wear months down the road.</p>"),
        ("Staking a corner-lot rental's grading before material even arrives",
         "<p>Because a {city} rental's vacancy window is often booked to the day, a corner lot's two grading exits get staked out and confirmed before rock or turf is ordered, rather than working that out once the crew is already on site with a truck idling in the driveway. Settling both exit points ahead of time means the crew arrives already knowing exactly how much material each side needs, which matters more inside a fixed multi-day window than it would on an open-ended owner-occupied schedule where a design question can be resolved on the fly without costing a booking.</p>"),
        ("Checking the fence-line fall with a hose before calling that edge done",
         "<p>The last step along a {city} rental's privacy-fence boundary is running a hose directly at the base rail and confirming water actually moves away from it rather than sitting against the fence, checking that the small extra fall built into the base at that edge is doing what it was graded to do. This gets checked specifically at the fence line, separately from testing the rest of the yard's drainage, since a fence rail sitting in water is a slower, less obvious failure than a puddle in open turf and easy to miss during a general walkthrough that only checks the middle of the yard.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Warning guests who have never stood on hot turf before",
         "<p>A family visiting {city} on vacation has no local habit of checking turf temperature before letting kids run across it barefoot, unlike a homeowner who learns quickly. Full summer sun pushes the surface to 120 to 150°F, and a rinse drops that 30 to 50 degrees within minutes, but that only helps if someone tells the guest to do it. A line in the house manual about hosing the yard before pool time, left where an out-of-town family will actually read it, does more good on a rental than the same fact would on a house where the residents already know it.</p>"),
        ("The upstairs balcony slider nobody thought to check",
         "<p>Two-story rental homes common in {city}'s resort communities often have a balcony slider on the upper level facing the backyard, and a low-emissivity coating on that glass can reflect concentrated sunlight down onto turf below just as readily as a ground-floor pool-cage window can, since polyethylene softens around 175 to 200°F either way. That upper window is easy to overlook during a site walk focused on ground-level glass. Checking the full height of a rental home's rear wall, not just the sliders at yard level, catches a reflection source that a single-story-focused walk would miss entirely.</p>"),
        ("Who actually schedules the arborist letter on an absentee property?",
         "<p>A mature oak shading part of a {city} rental yard still falls under the same drip-line rule as any other lot: no turf inside the root zone without a certified arborist's letter. The complication on a rental is less about the rule itself and more about who remembers to arrange it when the owner lives out of state and the property manager is focused on bookings, not landscaping compliance. Flagging a tree's drip line during the initial site visit, before quoting the job, keeps that step from getting missed simply because nobody local was thinking about it.</p>"),
        ("The ten-foot setback on a lakefront rental lot",
         "<p>A meaningful share of {city}'s short-term rental homes back onto a community lake or canal, and turf on those lots still has to stay at least ten feet from the water's ordinary edge unless a seawall or bulkhead already runs along the bank. That setback does not shrink just because the lot is a rental rather than an owner-occupied home, and a wide-open lake view is often exactly the feature a listing photo wants to show, which makes it tempting to run turf closer to the water than the standard allows. Holding the line at ten feet keeps the job compliant regardless of how good the view would look otherwise.</p>"),
        ("How a base problem turns into a bad review before anyone visits",
         "<p>A base built too shallow or without enough compaction under a {city} rental will fail the same way it would on any property, holding water or settling unevenly, but the first sign of trouble is often a guest's photo in a review rather than anything an owner notices directly. Weeks can pass between when a soft spot starts and when a booking platform shows the complaint publicly, since nobody local is walking the yard between stays. Getting the two to four inches of washed rock right at installation avoids a problem that, on this kind of property, gets discovered in public rather than privately.</p>"),
        ("When drifted infill becomes the neighboring rental's problem",
         "<p>In a tightly packed {city} rental subdivision, infill that washes off a poorly graded yard during a storm has less distance to travel before it lands in the neighboring property's turf or landscaping than it would on a spread-out lot, and a complaint from next door is often how a distant owner first learns their base or edge has a drainage problem at all. Handling that complaint remotely, sometimes through a property manager relaying a message secondhand, takes longer than an owner noticing the same issue firsthand would. A properly graded edge that keeps infill inside the property line avoids the situation from ever starting.</p>"),
        ("The swale a remote site plan misses on a corner-lot rental",
         "<p>An owner or investor planning a {city} rental's turf conversion from out of state sometimes works from a satellite image or a listing photo rather than an in-person walk of the lot, and a corner property's side-street swale is exactly the kind of detail that's hard to spot without standing on the ground. Ordering a full-yard job based on that remote view risks including square footage inside a swale the state's rule doesn't allow to be turfed at all. A site visit by whoever's actually doing the installation, confirming both frontages before finalizing the layout, catches this regardless of how the original plan was drawn up.</p>"),
        ("A buried septic lid discovered during a turnover week",
         "<p>If a {city} rental's septic lid isn't kept clear during turf work, the discovery often happens at the worst possible time: a scheduled pump-out arriving during a tight turnover window between guests, unable to locate the access point without probing through finished turf. That delay can push against the next booking's start time in a way a missed lid on an owner-occupied home never would, since there's no flexible day to reschedule around. Marking and rechecking the lid's location before and after any turf work protects the pump-out schedule as much as it protects the turf itself.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("What the cleaning crew checks between every turnover",
         "<p>On most {city} rentals, whoever cleans the house between guests is also the only regular set of eyes on the yard, so a quick pass with the blower to clear storm debris and a rinse of anything spilled near the pool cage becomes part of the standard turnover rather than a separate maintenance visit. Growing resort corridors nearby sometimes track construction dust onto a yard that would otherwise stay clean between bookings, which is one more reason that turnover-day glance matters more here than on a house with residents walking the yard daily anyway.</p>"),
        ("Should a deeper brush follow a listing refresh instead of a fixed date?",
         "<p>Rather than brushing a {city} rental yard's main path on a flat schedule, it works better tied to whenever the listing photos get refreshed, since a flattened path shows up clearly in a new photo set in a way it would not in daily use nobody photographs. A property gearing up for a rate increase or a seasonal relisting benefits from a brush and a look at infill levels right before new photos go up, rather than on a calendar date that might land the week after a busy stretch has already worn the path down again.</p>"),
        ("Checking edges after a large group books the house",
         "<p>A {city} rental that just hosted a bachelor party or a big family reunion is worth a closer look at the turf's perimeter than a property coming off a quiet weekend for two, since more people moving furniture, coolers, and inflatable pool gear across the yard puts more stress on an edge in a few days than a typical stay does in a month. Tying an edge check to booking size rather than a fixed calendar date catches the wear that actually happened instead of checking on a schedule that might miss the one stay that mattered.</p>"),
        ("Getting infill topped off before the season, not during it",
         "<p>A property manager's standing vendor for a {city} rental portfolio does better scheduling an infill check ahead of the busiest booking stretch of the year than waiting for a fixed quarterly date that might land in the middle of it. Topping off zeolite or silica sand before demand peaks means the yard is at full depth exactly when the most guests will be on it, rather than catching up on infill after a season's worth of heavy traffic and hose rinsing has already thinned it out. The timing matters as much as the frequency here.</p>"),
        ("Keeping the septic lid reachable on a lot nobody watches daily",
         "<p>Many {city} rental subdivisions still run on septic rather than sewer, and the state's turf standard requires that tank's access lid stay reachable for a pump-out even after turf goes down around it. On an owner-occupied home, someone usually notices if infill or a repaired edge has crept over that lid; on a rental, it can go unnoticed until a scheduled pump-out truck shows up and the crew cannot find it. Confirming the lid's location stays clear during any infill top-up or edge repair matters more here specifically because nobody local is checking on it between service visits.</p>"),
        ("Building a handoff packet before the crew's truck leaves",
         "<p>A {city} rental benefits from a short written handoff packet on installation day: infill type and rate, backing spec, and a photo marking the septic lid's location if the property has one, kept with whatever records the property manager already maintains rather than living only in the original owner's memory. Any vendor called out later, whether for a storm check or a routine top-up, works faster with that reference than starting from scratch on a property they've never seen before. This costs a few minutes on install day and saves considerably more the first time something needs troubleshooting years later.</p>"),
        ("Comparing a rental's two-year wear against a similar property",
         "<p>An owner or manager with more than one {city} rental can use a two-year mark to compare how a given yard's infill depth, seam condition, and edge anchoring have held up against a similar property installed around the same time, which is a more useful comparison than judging a single yard against its own installation-day condition alone. A property wearing noticeably faster than a comparable one in the same portfolio, despite similar product and base specs, usually points to something specific about that location, heavier booking volume, a shadier or wetter lot, worth investigating rather than assuming it's simply due for maintenance.</p>"),
        ("Why a turnover check after a storm matters more than a routine one",
         "<p>Between June and September, a {city} rental's between-turnover check should specifically look for anything a recent storm left behind, a lifted edge, a soft spot, drifted infill, rather than just the usual sweep for cosmetic debris, since nobody local is watching the yard on the days between a storm and the next scheduled cleaning. A routine turnover check built around cosmetics alone can miss a drainage problem that's been sitting for a week or more by the time anyone looks closely. Flagging the property for an extra look any time a significant storm has passed since the last turnover closes that gap.</p>"),
    ],
}
